INT. RAILWAY TELEGRAPH OFFICE, SILVER GAP, COLORADO - NIGHT (1893)

A single oil lamp. Frost feathering the window glass. The key chatters on its brass base like a nervous bird, and a stove ticks in the corner, losing its argument with the cold.

MABEL HOLLOWAY (30s, ink on her cuffs, hair pinned tight enough to hurt) sits at the desk, one hand on the key, the other flat on a stack of unsent forms. Across from her, REVEREND ISAIAH THORNE (60s, gaunt, black coat gone green at the seams) stands with his hat held against his chest as though it might be asked for.

THORNE
It has been four hours.

MABEL
I know how long it's been, Reverend.

THORNE
Then you know the whole town is gathered at the church, waiting to be told something.

MABEL
Then they'll wait.

The key stops. She lifts her hand from it as if it had grown hot.

THORNE
Miss Holloway. A telegram arrives from the mine company. You read it. And now you will not tell me what it says.

MABEL
It isn't addressed to you.

THORNE
It is addressed to forty families.

Mabel looks at the paper under her palm. She does not move her hand.

MABEL
It's addressed to the superintendent. He isn't here to receive it.

THORNE
Then I will carry it to him.

MABEL
He's in the shaft.

Silence. The stove ticks. Thorne lowers his hat, slowly, onto the desk.

THORNE
Ah.

The door bangs open. SHERIFF GUS ARMBRUSTER (50s, mustache full of snow, a badge he never polishes) stamps in, breathing hard, and shoves the door shut with his shoulder.

ARMBRUSTER
Lights are out on the whole ridge. Somebody's cut the line past the trestle.

MABEL
It wasn't cut. It was buried.

ARMBRUSTER
Buried?

MABEL
Slide came down at nine. I felt the wire go slack under my hand.

ARMBRUSTER
And you didn't think to say?

MABEL
I said to Reverend Thorne. Four hours ago. He didn't ask what I meant.

Thorne looks at the floor. Armbruster takes off his gloves one finger at a time, then looks at the stove, and then at the key.

ARMBRUSTER
Can you raise anyone?

MABEL
I've been trying since nine.

She taps out a short burst. Nothing. She taps again. The key answers with a faint, dry, stuttering click, as though someone far away had leaned on the other end by accident.

The three of them go still.

DISPATCHER (V.O.)
(thin, crackling, from the sounder)
...Silver Gap... Silver Gap, this is Division at Leadville... come back...

Mabel seizes the key. Armbruster is at her shoulder in two strides. Thorne's lips begin to move, soundless.

MABEL
(tapping as she speaks)
Division, this is Holloway at Gap. Line is restored. Repeat, line is restored.

DISPATCHER (V.O.)
(fading in)
...Holloway, we had you for dead. Report conditions...

ARMBRUSTER
Tell him about the slide. Tell him about the mine.

MABEL
I'm telling him.

DISPATCHER (V.O.)
...say again, conditions at Number Four shaft...

Mabel's hand hesitates above the key. She glances at Thorne, then at the paper on the desk, then back at the key.

THORNE
Read it to him, Miss Holloway.

MABEL
Reverend--

THORNE
Read it. Whatever it says. Better he hears it from us than from a stranger.

Mabel slides the paper out from under her palm, finally. Unfolds it. Her eyes travel across it once. She folds it again.

MABEL
Fourteen men underground at the time of the slide.

ARMBRUSTER
Fourteen.

MABEL
Superintendent among them. Rescue party left Leadville at six. They are expected at dawn.

The door opens a third time. A gust, a flurry of snow, and ROSALIND PEMBERTON (20s, bare-headed, carrying a lantern and a baby wrapped in a shawl) stands on the threshold. Her hair is wet with melt, and her face is entirely composed, which is worse than if it were not.

ROSALIND
Mr. Armbruster. They said you'd be here.

ARMBRUSTER
Mrs. Pemberton. You shouldn't be out in this.

ROSALIND
My husband is Number Four's night boss. I want to know if anyone has heard from Number Four.

Nobody answers. Thorne takes a step toward her. Armbruster sets his gloves on the desk. Mabel keeps her eyes on the key.

ROSALIND (CONT'D)
(quietly)
I've been in this town six years. I know what it sounds like when no one is answering.

MABEL
Mrs. Pemberton, the line has only just come back. We know almost nothing.

ROSALIND
Then tell me almost.

Mabel stands, and for the first time her voice cracks and she does not hide it.

MABEL
Fourteen men were below. The rescue party leaves Leadville at dawn. That's all that is known.

ROSALIND
Fourteen.

She looks down at the baby, who is asleep. She adjusts the shawl with great care.

ROSALIND (CONT'D)
Then I will wait with the others. Reverend, will you take me to the church?

THORNE
(hat in hand again)
It would be an honor.

DISPATCHER (V.O.)
...Gap, do you copy... Gap...

Mabel sits. Her fingers find the key. Rosalind and Thorne go out into the snow, and Armbruster holds the door for them, watching their lantern shrink up the hill.

ARMBRUSTER
(to himself)
Dawn.

He shuts the door. The stove ticks. The key begins, once more, to chatter.
